-- title:
--   Powers of an invertible ideal sheaf are invertible
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme (in a fixed universe) and let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $X$, i.e. an element of `X.IdealSheafData`. Assume $I$ satisfies `IsInvertible`: for every point $x \in X$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x$ lying in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ which is a non-zero-divisor and generates the ideal attached to $I$ on that affine basic open, that is $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,(X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f) = (g)$. Then for every natural number $n$ the $n$-th power $I^n$, formed with respect to the multiplication of ideal sheaf data, again satisfies `IsInvertible`: locally on suitable affine basic opens it is generated by a single non-zero-divisor. In particular the case $n = 0$ is included, where $I^0$ is the unit ideal sheaf datum.
--
--   This is the statement that powers of a locally principal ideal sheaf generated by non-zero-divisors — an effective Cartier divisor in the usual terminology — remain of this type. It is used throughout the construction of divisors and associated line bundles on curves, for instance to form the sheaves $\mathcal{O}(\pm r D)$ attached to a multiple of a section or a point.
